  abstract = {Neural representations often change over time, in spite of stable behavior. This phenomenon, termed representational drift, challenges the established concept of stable memory engrams. While existing theories focus either on potential mechanistic causes of drift or on ways to compensate for it, the computational function of drift remains unclear. Here, we propose that representational drift may be a signature of memory consolidation.},
